Obsessive-compulsive disorder
Mental Health & Substance Use
Intrusive egodystonic thoughts, Repetitive ritualistic behaviors, Time-consuming compulsions

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-035
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute panic attack and bleeding hands resulting from an uncontrollable, hours-long handwashing compulsion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute crisis marked by severe hyperventilation, extreme distress, and bleeding from the hands due to compulsive washing that the patient could not stop. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess the dermatological damage, break the severe panic cycle, and provide immediate pharmacological and behavioral interruption of the compulsion prior to regular hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I touched the doorknob and I know I have a deadly bacteria on me. I've washed my hands thirty times with bleach, they are bleeding, but I can't stop the feeling that I'm contaminated."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports chronic, recurrent and persistent intrusive thoughts and images of severe contamination causing marked anxiety. The patient makes frantic attempts to ignore, suppress, or neutralize these obsessions with some other thought or action. This manifests as repetitive behaviors (handwashing) that the individual feels driven to perform. There is a rigid adherence to rules applied to compulsions to prevent dreaded events (e.g., must wash exactly 7 times). These time-consuming rituals take up more than 4 hours per day. Crucially, the patient displays clear egodystonic recognition that the beliefs or compulsions are excessive and irrational, yet feels powerless to stop. Today's acute crisis resulted in severe dermatologic manifestations (cracked, bleeding skin) from using household bleach to wash. The condition causes significant distress and impairment, including avoidance of all social situations.
Functional Status: Currently trapped in the bathroom, functionally paralyzed by the compulsion.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe chemical burns and secondary skin infection; severe psychological exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/88 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Highly distressed, hyperventilating, pacing, holding hands away from the body.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia. Respiratory exam shows rapid, shallow breathing. Integumentary exam of the bilateral hands and distal forearms reveals severe contact dermatitis: skin is erythematous, deeply cracked, with multiple areas of superficial pinpoint bleeding and extreme dryness. No deep ulcerations or signs of systemic sepsis. Mental state examination is characterized by severe intrusive egodystonic thoughts of contamination, active repetitive ritualistic behaviors, and immense distress regarding the time-consuming compulsions.
Environmental Observations: The bathroom sink is surrounded by various harsh chemical cleaners, including industrial bleach.
Medication Reconciliation: Prescribed Sertraline 100mg, but reports poor compliance over the last month.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Obsessive-compulsive disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Acute compulsion crisis with chemical dermatitis)
Differentials:

Delusional Disorder (Somatic Type) - Ruled out/unlikely because the patient retains insight; they explicitly state, "I know it's crazy to wash with bleach, but the fear won't stop." (Egodystonic).

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to the specific, ritualistic nature of the compulsions directly tied to specific intrusive images, rather than pervasive, free-floating worry.

Primary Irritant Contact Dermatitis - While present, it is entirely secondary to the primary psychiatric pathology of OCD, not an independent dermatological disease.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in an acute state of severe panic and physical self-harm (chemical burns) secondary to an uncontrollable OCD compulsion. Telephone triage correctly identified the bleeding hands and extreme distress. Urgent after-hours medical assessment was essential to physically evaluate the extent of the chemical burns, remove the hazardous chemicals, and administer an acute anxiolytic to chemically break the panic cycle driving the compulsion. Delaying care until the morning would have likely resulted in continued bleach use, leading to full-thickness chemical burns and a highly elevated risk of severe infection.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Assisted the patient in washing hands gently with warm water and basic soap to remove residual bleach. Applied a thick layer of emollient cream (e.g., Sorbolene) and wrapped hands in light cotton bandages to physically block further washing. Administered oral Diazepam 5mg STAT to immediately downregulate the severe panic driving the compulsion. Expected investigation results: Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale indicating severe severity.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement "Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) Delay." Instruct the patient that when the urge to wash arises, they must delay the action by exactly 10 minutes. During this delay, they must sit with hands in pockets. Reward the successful delay with verbal self-praise and the knowledge that the anxiety naturally decreases slightly over those 10 minutes. This uses operant conditioning to build tolerance to the distress without performing the compulsion.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to a specialized OCD psychology clinic for formal ERP therapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): All harsh chemicals removed from the bathroom and secured in a locked cabinet.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours for a skin check and to discuss optimizing SSRI therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the hands become severely swollen, hot, or ooze yellow pus, or if the patient experiences an uncontrollable urge to ingest the cleaning chemicals.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute crisis intervention provided; OCD is a severe condition requiring specialized, high-dose SSRI therapy and intensive psychology.
Clinical Course: The skin will heal with moisturization and the cessation of bleach use. The obsessions will persist but can be managed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the pain in your hands becomes unbearable, or if red streaks begin moving up your arms, seek medical care immediately.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain's "danger alarm" is malfunctioning, telling you there is a threat when there isn't one. The medication will temporarily mute the alarm so you can stop washing. The bleach is causing real physical damage to your skin.
Actionable Steps: Keep the cotton bandages on your hands tonight. Do not use any chemicals other than gentle soap. Restart your prescribed Sertraline tomorrow.

